# Building a counterfactual state: synthetic control and Proposition 99
next: {"title":"Reading a null: power, precision, and the Oregon blood-sugar result","href":"https://drawcast.app/#gh=hmelberg/dcast/causal-inference-in-economics-evidence-from-health-and-health-care/reading-a-null-power-precision-and-the-oregon-blood-sugar-result.cast"}

## One Treated Unit: Proposition 99
voice: female
level: advanced
domain: {"x":[1970,2000],"y":[0,145]}

    axes axes x_label Year y_label "Cigarette packs sold per person" hidden true
    curve ca_pre #b5482e expr "123 - 1.6*(x-1970)" x_from 1970 x_to 1988 hidden true
    label label_ca "California" above #b5482e attach_to ca_pre hidden true
    curve ca_post #b5482e expr "94 - 4.4*(x-1988)" x_from 1988 x_to 2000 hidden true

    draw axes

Over four parts we'll build a California that never passed Proposition 99 — because without it, a state can't tell whether its own tax worked, and this red line is what has to be imitated: packs sold per person per year.
    draw ca_pre label_ca

    pause 0.3

In November 1988 California's voters passed Proposition 99: twenty-five cents of extra tax on every pack, with the revenue earmarked for anti-smoking campaigns.
    dot prop99 at.x 1988 at.y 94 guides true
    label label_prop99 "Proposition 99, Nov 1988" above-left attach_to prop99

Sales keep sliding afterwards — from about ninety packs a year down to roughly forty by 2000.
    draw ca_post

The awkward part is on the left: the decline was already under way before anyone voted.
    highlight ca_pre effect glow

    pause 0.4

The instinct from lecture eleven is a comparison group — the average of the rest of the United States.
    curve us_pre #2f6b8f expr "130 - 1.2*(x-1970)" x_from 1970 x_to 1988
    curve us_post #2f6b8f expr "108 - 3.0*(x-1988)" x_from 1988 x_to 2000
    label label_us "Rest of the U.S. (average)" above #2f6b8f attach_to us_pre

And parallel trends fails in plain sight: before 1988 California already starts lower and falls faster.
    point at.x 1979 at.y 103 gesture underline

That difference-in-differences would hand Proposition 99 credit for a drop that was happening anyway.
    mark strike_us target label_us kind strike

    pause 0.4

So widen the search instead of settling for one comparison.
    erase us_pre us_post label_us strike_us parallel true

Thirty-eight states ran no large tobacco-control program of their own in these years — the donor pool, a few of them shown here.
    curve d1 thin #8f887c expr "140 - 1.9*(x-1970)"
    curve d2 thin #8f887c expr "118 - 1.2*(x-1970)"
    curve d3 thin #8f887c expr "128 - 2.4*(x-1970)"
    curve d4 thin #8f887c expr "108 - 0.9*(x-1970)"
    curve d5 thin #8f887c expr "134 - 1.5*(x-1970)"
    curve d6 thin #8f887c expr "116 - 1.7*(x-1970)"
    label label_donors "38 donor states" above-right #8f887c attach_to d1
    parallel true

And not one of them traces California: the closest match still differs in prices, demographics, and tastes.
    focus ca_pre ca_post

What the question needs is this dashed path — California with D equal to zero after 1988, a potential outcome that with a single treated unit is never observed.
    curve cf dashed #8a5fa8 expr "94 - 2.6*(x-1988)" x_from 1988 x_to 2000
    label label_cf "Y_CA(0) = ?" above #8a5fa8 attach_to cf

No state is that path. A weighted combination of thirty-eight of them might be.
    highlight cf effect glow

The idea arrived in 2003, when Abadie and Gardeazabal assembled a synthetic Basque Country to price the economic cost of terrorism; California's tobacco tax became its most famous application.
    delivery soft

So the design changes: we stop picking a comparison state and start constructing one — and next, why a blend beats both the closest match and the plain average.
    mark mark_cf target label_cf kind box
    delivery grave

## Choosing the Weights
voice: female
level: advanced
domain: {"x":[1970,1990],"y":[60,140]}

Now that we have seen why a blend beats any single donor state, the weights themselves have to come from somewhere — and they come entirely from California's own record before 1989.
    text pred_head "Matched over 1970–1988" x 268 y 650 font_size 28

Abadie, Diamond and Hainmueller had the algorithm match four predictors of smoking — the retail price of cigarettes, income per capita, the share aged fifteen to twenty-four, and beer consumption — plus California's actual sales in 1975, 1980 and 1988.
    text p1 "Retail cigarette price" x 268 y 592 font_size 25
    text p2 "Income per capita" x 268 y 548 font_size 25
    text p3 "Share aged 15–24" x 268 y 504 font_size 25
    text p4 "Beer consumption" x 268 y 460 font_size 25
    text p5 "Sales in 1975, 1980, 1988" x 268 y 416 font_size 25
    parallel true

Then one line of optimization: search over all weights that are non-negative and sum to one, and keep whichever set makes that pre-treatment distance smallest.
    text obj1 "Choose weights w ≥ 0 with Σ w = 1" x 500 y 250 font_size 26
    text obj2 "minimizing the pre-1988 distance to California" x 500 y 205 font_size 26

    pause 0.4

The winner: Utah with a third of the weight, Nevada a quarter, Montana a fifth, Colorado a sixth, Connecticut the small remainder — synthetic California is those five states, in those proportions.
    text w_head "Synthetic California" #2f6b8f x 742 y 650 font_size 28
    text w1 "Utah   0.334" #2f6b8f x 742 y 592 font_size 25
    text w2 "Nevada   0.234" #2f6b8f x 742 y 548 font_size 25
    text w3 "Montana   0.199" #2f6b8f x 742 y 504 font_size 25
    text w4 "Colorado   0.164" #2f6b8f x 742 y 460 font_size 25
    text w5 "Connecticut   0.069" #2f6b8f x 742 y 416 font_size 25

The other thirty-three donors get exactly zero — because weights cannot go negative, the solution comes out sparse: a handful in, the rest out.
    text w0 "Other 33 states   0" #8f887c x 742 y 352 font_size 25
    mark mark_zero target w0 kind box

And nothing here was picked for resemblance — Utah and Nevada look nothing like California politically or economically; they are in the blend only because together they reproduce its smoking history.
    highlight w1 w2 w3 w4 w5 effect glow

    pause 0.5

    clear

    axes ax x_label Year y_label "Packs sold per capita"

Here is the history that had to be reproduced: California sold about a hundred and twenty-three packs per person in 1970, and about ninety by 1988.
    curve ca_curve #b5482e expr "123 + 1.2*(x-1970) - 0.18*(x-1970)^2"
    label label_ca "California" below #b5482e attach_to ca_curve

The simple average of the thirty-eight donor states starts in the same place, then drifts — sitting more than twenty packs above California by 1988.
    curve rest_curve dashed #8f887c expr "122 + 0.6*(x-1970) - 0.055*(x-1970)^2"
    label label_rest "Average of 38 states" above #8f887c attach_to rest_curve

The weighted blend does what the average cannot: it tracks California almost year for year, across all nineteen pre-treatment years.
    curve synth_curve dashed #2f6b8f expr "124 + 1.0*(x-1970) - 0.174*(x-1970)^2"
    label label_synth "Synthetic California" left #2f6b8f attach_to synth_curve

    pause 0.5

Proposition 99 passed in November 1988, raising the cigarette tax by twenty-five cents a pack from the first of January 1989.
    arrow prop_line dashed #8f887c from.x 1988 from.y 137 to.x 1988 to.y 64
    label label_prop "Proposition 99 (1988)" left #8f887c attach_to prop_line

Everything left of that line can be checked against reality; everything right of it is an assumption we can never test.
    label fit_note "The only testable claim" above-left attach_to synth_curve
    mark mark_fit target fit_note kind box

So the fit carries the whole credibility argument — and Abadie's own advice is blunt: when no combination of donors tracks the pre-period closely, you should not run the method at all.
    focus ca_curve synth_curve delivery grave

The weights are chosen by fitting a past we can see, and the counterfactual is only ever as believable as that fit.

